---
import { getCollection, getEntry } from "astro:content";
import StoryLayout from "@layouts/StoryLayout.astro";
import Chapter from "@components/story/Chapter.astro";
import ProofRow from "@components/ProofRow.astro";
import WaitlistForm from "@components/WaitlistForm.astro";
import { byId } from "@lib/chapters";
import { HARVEST_LOOP, HARVEST_PILLARS, HARVEST_TERMS } from "@lib/career";

const pick = async (...ids: string[]) =>
  (await Promise.all(ids.map((id) => getEntry("proof", id)))).filter(Boolean) as any[];

const crustProof = await pick("elt-pipelines");
const mantleProof = await pick(
  "rag-support-chatbot",
  "peft-quantization",
  "llm-as-a-judge-evals",
  "uscs-soil-classification",
  "async-task-backend"
);
const stabilityProof = await pick("cortex-axon-synapse");
const ascentProof = await pick("oasis", "fde-pods");

const allProof = (await getCollection("proof")).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order
);

const essays = (await getCollection("essays")).sort(
  (a, b) => (a.data.flagship ? -1 : b.data.flagship ? 1 : 0)
);
---

<StoryLayout>
  <!-- ══ 1 · PREP ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       The anti-arrogance inoculation, and it goes first. "I am made of the same
       material as everyone else" is the most disarming opening available to a
       man with this CV. -->
  <Chapter
    chapter={byId("prep")}
    visual="A single point of specular light above a flat graphite sheet in wireframe, its layers slipping past one another."
  >
    <p class="mono ident">
      A. M. MADANI &middot; AI SOLUTIONS ARCHITECT<br />
      CTO, SMARTWAY SOLUTIONS &middot; GERMANY &middot; EU
    </p>

    <p class="lead">
      Graphite and diamond are the same element, atom for atom. One is worth
      nothing.
    </p>

    <p>
      The difference is how deep it went, how hard it was pressed, and how fast
      it got out. Nothing about the material. Everything about the process.
    </p>

    <p>
      I build privacy-first AI systems for European enterprises. This page is
      the process, not the r&eacute;sum&eacute;.
    </p>

    <p class="cta-row">
      <a href="#crust" class="cta">Begin the descent &darr;</a>
      <a href="/dossier/" class="cta cta-quiet">Skip to the evidence &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ══ 2 · CRUST ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Chapter
    chapter={byId("crust")}
    visual="Horizontal strata accumulating from above, each labelled with a year, as graphite sheets stack and their interlayer gaps close."
  >
    <p>
      Mahalat, then Astrakhan. A B.Sc. in computer science, then a master&rsquo;s
      in applied mathematics and informatics, and three years of freelance data
      engineering in between and after &mdash; pipelines, ingestion, validation.
      The unglamorous layer.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is the part nobody puts on a conference slide. It is also the part
      that decides everything above it. You cannot reason about a model&rsquo;s
      output if you cannot vouch for what went into it, and vouching for what
      went into it is months of work that looks like nothing.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Depth is not optional, and it is boring.</strong> Every layer of
      crust has to be passed through at the speed the crust allows.
    </p>

    <div class="wide"><ProofRow entries={crustProof} /></div>

    <p class="payload">
      Most enterprise AI programmes fail in this layer. They start at the model.
      The model is the last thing you choose.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ══ 3 · MANTLE ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Chapter
    chapter={byId("mantle")}
    visual="Slow convection cells turning in the background as the palette warms; the stacked graphite sheets begin to buckle."
  >
    <p>
      Two years at VASL, first as an AI engineer and then as head of AI. Retrieval
      systems, async infrastructure, model compression, evaluation frameworks,
      multi-agent pipelines for geotechnical classification. Volume, variety, and
      not enough time.
    </p>

    <p>
      Heat alone does not make anything valuable. Applied to carbon without
      structure, it just burns. The years that look like raw thermal load only
      become worth something if something holds their shape while the load is
      applied.
    </p>

    <p>
      What held was the <strong>Minimum Quality Floor</strong> &mdash; a bar
      below which nothing ships, no matter how urgent. That sounds like a brake.
      It is the opposite. A 60% cut in GPU spend is not frugality; it is the
      discipline that made a 300% increase in deployment frequency survivable
      rather than reckless.
    </p>

    <p>
      Constraint is generative. Evals are what let you move fast without finding
      out six weeks later that you moved fast in the wrong direction.
    </p>

    <div class="wide"><ProofRow entries={mantleProof} /></div>

    <p class="payload">
      If your team cannot tell you whether last week&rsquo;s change made the
      system better, you do not have a velocity problem. You have an
      instrumentation problem, and velocity is the symptom.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ══ 4 · STABILITY FIELD ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       The pivot. The paragraph that describes the diamond stability field also
       describes Cortex-Axon-Synapse: the architecture and the metaphor are the
       same diagram. -->
  <Chapter
    chapter={byId("stability")}
    visual="The vignette closing inward; buckled sheets collapse and snap into a first cubic cell."
  >
    <p>
      Dubai. Lead forward deployed engineer and product architect at Mistix AI,
      in the rooms where seven-figure decisions actually get made.
    </p>

    <p>
      Below 150 kilometres there is a region where carbon has exactly one stable
      arrangement. Above that line, the same atoms under the same heat relax back
      into graphite. The pressure is not incidental to the diamond. It is the
      only reason it exists.
    </p>

    <p>
      What I built there has the same shape. <strong>Cortex</strong> plans,
      <strong>Axon</strong> executes deterministically, <strong>Synapse</strong>
      guards the boundary &mdash; three layers, each rigid, each bonded to the
      next in a fixed relationship. Uncertainty is quarantined at the top where
      it can be measured. Nothing below it is allowed to improvise.
    </p>

    <p>
      That is <strong>Architect First</strong>: structure, interfaces and system
      design determine speed, quality and reuse before a line ships. Get the
      lattice right and onboarding the next enterprise stops being an engineering
      project and becomes an integration one. The difference between those two
      words is about nine weeks and a great deal of money.
    </p>

    <div class="wide"><ProofRow entries={stabilityProof} /></div>

    <p class="payload">
      Nobody signs a seven-figure contract because the demo was good. They sign
      because the architecture told them what happens in month eighteen.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ══ 5 · LATTICE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       The intellectual centrepiece: a lattice is the same cell repeated, a
       methodology is the same loop repeated. Capture A lands here, at the point
       of maximum comprehension. -->
  <Chapter
    chapter={byId("lattice")}
    visual="A diamond cubic lattice assembling cell by cell; each of the six loop stages illuminates one bond family."
  >
    <p>
      Nature takes between one and three and a half billion years to do this,
      because nature has no method. It has time instead.
    </p>

    <p>
      A lattice is not complicated. It is one cell, repeated, with absolute
      fidelity, until the repetition itself becomes the hardest natural substance
      there is. Nothing in a diamond is clever. Everything in it is consistent.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The Harvest Methodology</strong> is the same claim about work: not
      a smarter loop, the <em>same</em> loop, closed and repeated until the
      repetition compounds.
    </p>

    <p class="loop mono">
      {HARVEST_LOOP.join("  →  ")}
    </p>

    <dl class="pillars wide">
      {
        HARVEST_PILLARS.map((p) => (
          <div>
            <dt>{p.name}</dt>
            <dd>{p.statement}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p>
      Bets are selected by <strong>ICE</strong> rather than by whoever argued
      most persuasively. Work runs through customer-proximate{" "}
      <strong>Deployment Pods</strong> with one owner each. Every delivery
      contributes to a <strong>Backbone</strong> of reusable IP, which is the
      only mechanism by which the second engagement is cheaper than the first.
    </p>

    <dl class="terms wide">
      {
        HARVEST_TERMS.map((t) => (
          <div>
            <dt class="mono">{t.term}</dt>
            <dd>{t.def}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p class="payload">
      A method is what you use instead of time. If your organisation is waiting
      to get good at AI, it is spending the one resource the geology does not
      sell.
    </p>

    <WaitlistForm
      placement="lattice"
      eyebrow="// THE HARVEST FIELD CARD"
      heading="The methodology on one page."
      blurb="The loop, the ICE scoring sheet, and the Minimum Quality Floor checklist — one page, made to be printed and pinned to a wall."
      button="Send the card"
    />
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ══ 6 · KIMBERLITE ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       The most persuasive forty seconds on the site, because the claim is
       counterintuitive and physics makes it irrefutable. -->
  <Chapter
    chapter={byId("ascent")}
    visual="Every direction reverses; the lattice fractures into facets and the facets fly upward, then stop dead at the surface."
  >
    <p>
      Germany. CTO at Smartway Solutions. Jira dismantled and replaced with an
      embedded forward-deployed engineer model, pods framed under Harvest, Oasis
      shipped, documentation cycles compressed tenfold.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here is the part of the geology that nobody expects.
    </p>

    <p>
      A diamond that forms at 190 kilometres and rises slowly does not arrive as
      a diamond. It reverts. Graphite is the stable form at the surface, and a
      leisurely ascent gives the carbon exactly the time it needs to relax back
      into something worthless. The stones that survive are the ones carried up
      in hours by a kimberlite eruption moving fast enough to outrun the
      chemistry.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The violence of the extraction is what preserves the value created
      under pressure.</strong> Speed is not the opposite of quality here. It is
      the precondition for it.
    </p>

    <p>
      That is <strong>Deliver First</strong>, and it is why 10x is a structural
      claim rather than a boast. Value that takes eighteen months to surface is
      not slow value. It is a different, cheaper substance wearing the same name.
    </p>

    <div class="wide"><ProofRow entries={ascentProof} /></div>

    <p class="payload">
      Every enterprise AI programme I have been handed was a slow ascent.
      Eighteen months of planning produces a very well-documented lump of
      graphite.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ══ 7 · THE CUT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Deliberately biography-free. This is the chapter the CFO reads; it should
       look like an instrument panel, not a hero section. -->
  <Chapter
    chapter={byId("cut")}
    visual="One stone in flat line-work, each facet labelled with a delivered system, with prismatic edges appearing for the only time on the site."
  >
    <p class="lead">
      Two stones of identical mass. One cut to the correct angles, one not. They
      differ in value by an order of magnitude.
    </p>

    <p>
      Diamond&rsquo;s critical angle is 24.4 degrees, which means light entering
      the crown will only return to the eye if the pavilion is cut to send it
      back. Miss the angle and the light passes straight through the bottom of
      the stone and is gone. Same carbon, same weight, same clarity &mdash; and
      most of the value on the floor, because of geometry.
    </p>

    <p>
      Same models. Same data. Same headcount. The architecture decides whether
      anything comes back out.
    </p>

    <div class="wide"><ProofRow entries={allProof} /></div>

    <p class="mono grid-note">
      Each row is individually linkable. Every number has a method note on its
      own page &mdash; <a href="/dossier/">or take the whole argument flat</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="payload">
      If two vendors quote you the same model and the same team size, you are not
      choosing between capabilities. You are choosing between geometries, and
      only one of them returns the light.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ══ 8 · LANDING ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Chapter
    chapter={byId("landing")}
    visual="The stone at rest. Nothing moves."
  >
    <p class="lead">
      Everything extracted becomes reusable. That is the last step of the loop,
      so it is the last chapter of the descent.
    </p>

    <section class="book" aria-labelledby="book-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">// MANUSCRIPT &middot; IN PROGRESS</p>
      <h3 class="book-title" id="book-title">Nothing Rare About Carbon</h3>
      <p class="book-sub">
        How pressure, speed and structure turn ordinary material into the most
        valuable thing you can hold &mdash; and why every enterprise AI
        programme fails on the way up.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Harvest Methodology, told as the story of being forced to invent it.
        Six years, four countries, $1.5M in delivered contracts, and one
        operating system for teams that have to ship faster than they can plan.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="excerpt">
        <p>
          The first thing I understood about the mantle is that it does not
          hurry, and it does not care. Pressure is not applied to carbon out of
          any interest in what the carbon becomes. It is simply the condition of
          being that far down.
        </p>
        <p>
          I have watched a great many engineering organisations try to
          manufacture that condition on purpose &mdash; the crunch, the war
          room, the quarter where everyone is asked to care more. It never
          produces diamonds. It produces exhausted people and a backlog with
          more items in it than when the quarter started.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the geology actually says is stranger and more useful. Pressure
          without structure destroys. Structure without pressure never
          consolidates. And both of them together, held for long enough, still
          produce nothing anybody will ever see &mdash; unless the last step is
          violent enough to bring it up before it reverts.
        </p>
        <p class="folio mono">&mdash; 041</p>
      </blockquote>

      <WaitlistForm
        placement="book"
        heading="Read the first chapter."
        blurb="One letter when there is something worth the ink. No sequence, no funnel."
        button="Request the first chapter"
      />
    </section>

    <section class="writing" aria-labelledby="writing-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">// WRITING</p>
      <h3 id="writing-title">Field notes &amp; deep dives</h3>
      <ul class="essays">
        {
          essays.map((e) => (
            <li>
              <a href={e.data.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                <span class="e-title">{e.data.title}</span>
                <span class="mono e-meta">
                  {e.data.flagship && <span class="flag">FLAGSHIP</span>}
                  <time datetime={e.data.date.toISOString()}>
                    {e.data.date.toLocaleDateString("en-GB", {
                      day: "numeric",
                      month: "short",
                      year: "numeric",
                    })}
                  </time>
                </span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">// CONTACT</p>
      <h3 id="contact-title">One line is enough.</h3>
      <p>
        Tell me what is broken and what it costs you per month. If I am not the
        right person I will say so in the first reply.
      </p>
      <p class="contact-line mono">
        <a href="mailto:amyrmahdy1@gmail.com">amyrmahdy1@gmail.com</a>
      </p>
      <p class="mono outbound">
        <a href="/dossier/">Dossier</a>
        <a href="/proof/">Proof</a>
        <a href="https://amyrmahdy.medium.com" rel="noopener noreferrer">Medium</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/amyrmahdy" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </Chapter>
</StoryLayout>

<style>
  .ident {
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 2;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .cta-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1.75rem; margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .cta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--accent);
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 40%, transparent);
    padding-bottom: 0.35rem;
    transition: border-color var(--dur-quick) var(--ease-facet);
  }
  .cta:hover { border-color: var(--accent); }
  .cta-quiet { color: var(--muted); border-bottom-color: var(--line); }
  .cta-quiet:hover { color: var(--fg); }

  .loop {
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-block: 2rem;
    padding-block: 1rem;
    border-block: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .pillars, .terms { display: grid; gap: 1.1rem; margin-block: 2rem; }
  .pillars div, .terms div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(8rem, 11rem) 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .pillars dt, .terms dt { font-weight: 600; color: var(--fg); }
  .terms dt { color: var(--accent); font-size: var(--t-micro); letter-spacing: 0.12em; padding-top: 0.25rem; }
  .pillars dd, .terms dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .grid-note {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .grid-note a { color: var(--accent); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }

  /* ── chapter 8 sections ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .book, .writing, .contact {
    margin-top: 4rem;
    padding-top: 2.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .book-title { font-size: var(--t-h2); margin-block: 0.9rem 0.75rem; }
  .book-sub {
    font-size: var(--t-lead);
    line-height: var(--lh-lead);
    color: var(--fg);
    max-width: 54ch;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* The text, not a 3D cover render. Only trade paperbacks need a mockup;
     the excerpt is the only thing that proves he can write. */
  .excerpt {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
    padding: 2rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .excerpt p { margin-block: 0 1.1em; }
  .excerpt p:first-of-type::first-letter {
    float: left;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 3.4em;
    line-height: 0.82;
    padding: 0.06em 0.1em 0 0;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .folio {
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 1.5rem !important;
  }

  .essays { list-style: none; margin: 1.75rem 0 0; padding: 0; }
  .essays li { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .essays li:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .essays a {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    padding-block: 1rem;
  }
  .essays a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  .e-title { font-size: var(--t-small); line-height: 1.45; }
  .e-meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .flag {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding: 0.15rem 0.4rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .contact h3 { font-size: var(--t-h3); margin-block: 0.9rem 0.75rem; }
  .contact-line { margin-top: 1.5rem; font-size: var(--t-lead); }
  .contact-line a { color: var(--accent); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .outbound {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .outbound a { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .outbound a:hover { color: var(--accent); }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .pillars div, .terms div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.35rem; }
    .essays a { flex-direction: column; gap: 0.4rem; }
  }
</style>
